From 8ea027e9ed90bf8fcfc643655a370adf863c50c6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Juan J. Sanchez" <jjsanchez@igalia.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 12:39:02 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] [WPE][Android] Apply the throttle state of auxiliary
 processes Need the bug URL (OOPS!).

Reviewed by NOBODY (OOPS!).

Before this change, the process throttler followed what each auxiliary process
was doing, such as showing a page, playing sound or loading, but only
RunningBoard acted on the state it chose. On WPE the throttlers of the web and
network processes were never connected and stayed in the foreground state: the
processes of hidden pages ran at the priority of the app, and were never asked
to free memory before going idle.

This change makes USE(PROCESS_ASSERTIONS) cover WPE on Android as well as
RunningBoard. On Android an assertion on an auxiliary process now holds a
priority through the default process manager, idle web processes take near
suspended assertions as with RunningBoard, and the throttlers of the web and
network processes are connected to their process when that manager ranks
processes. With a manager that does not, the throttlers stay unconnected, as
before. Nothing changes with RunningBoard.

Unlike RunningBoard, Android does not freeze the processes of an app apart from
the app itself, so a suspended process there keeps running.
HAVE(RUNNING_SUSPENDED_PROCESSES) says so. Where it is set, the network process
is not told when a web process is suspended, as it is with RunningBoard, so it
keeps serving the IndexedDB transactions of that process, and the network
process itself is never suspended, as on macOS, so storage keeps working.

No new tests (OOPS!).

